==================================================================
DAY 18 - PART B: LAB
Expose with NodePort and LoadBalancer, read the iptables rules
Time: 75-90 minutes
==================================================================

cd ~/k8s-journey/manifests/week3
k apply -f backend.yaml -f backend-svc.yaml


STEP 1: NodePort Service
------------------------
vi backend-nodeport.yaml

apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: backend-np
spec:
  type: NodePort
  selector:
    app: backend
  ports:
  - port: 80
    targetPort: 8080
    nodePort: 30080

k apply -f backend-nodeport.yaml
k get svc backend-np

PORT(S) shows 80:30080/TCP. It also has a ClusterIP.


STEP 2: Reach it through any node
---------------------------------
k get nodes -o wide          # INTERNAL-IP of each node (172.18.0.x)
for ip in $(k get nodes -o jsonpath='{.items[*].status.addresses[0].address}'); do
  echo -n "$ip -> "; curl -s $ip:30080
done

Every node answers, even nodes with no backend pod.
(Your host can reach 172.18.0.x because kind nodes are on a Docker
network. On a real cluster you'd use the node IPs.)


STEP 3: Read the iptables rules (kube-proxy)
--------------------------------------------
docker exec -it lab-worker bash

  iptables -t nat -L KUBE-SERVICES -n | grep backend
  iptables -t nat -L KUBE-NODEPORTS -n | grep 30080

Follow the chain. Copy the KUBE-SVC-XXXX name:
  iptables -t nat -L KUBE-SVC-XXXX -n
You'll see one line per endpoint with "statistic mode random
probability 0.333..." etc.

  iptables -t nat -L KUBE-SEP-YYYY -n
-> DNAT to a pod IP:8080

  exit

(If your cluster uses nftables mode: docker exec lab-worker nft list
ruleset | grep -A3 backend)

Check kube-proxy's mode:
  k logs -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-proxy --tail=50 | grep -i proxier


STEP 4: Scale and watch the probabilities change
------------------------------------------------
k scale deploy backend --replicas=4
docker exec lab-worker iptables -t nat -L KUBE-SVC-XXXX -n
Now 0.25, 0.333, 0.5, and the last one takes the remainder.


STEP 5: externalTrafficPolicy: Local
------------------------------------
k scale deploy backend --replicas=1
k get pods -l app=backend -o wide          # which node?
k patch svc backend-np -p '{"spec":{"externalTrafficPolicy":"Local"}}'
for ip in $(k get nodes -o jsonpath='{.items[*].status.addresses[0].address}'); do
  echo -n "$ip -> "; curl -s -m 2 $ip:30080 || echo "no answer"
done

Only the node that has the pod answers. Switch back:
  k patch svc backend-np -p '{"spec":{"externalTrafficPolicy":"Cluster"}}'
  k scale deploy backend --replicas=3


STEP 6: LoadBalancer (pending in kind)
--------------------------------------
k expose deploy backend --name=backend-lb --type=LoadBalancer --port=80 --target-port=8080
k get svc backend-lb

EXTERNAL-IP = <pending>. Nothing in kind creates load balancers.
Look: it already has a ClusterIP AND a NodePort.


STEP 7 (OPTIONAL): Real LoadBalancer in kind with cloud-provider-kind
---------------------------------------------------------------------
cloud-provider-kind is an official kind add-on that acts like a cloud
controller and gives LoadBalancer Services an IP.
  Download the binary for your OS from
    https://github.com/kubernetes-sigs/cloud-provider-kind/releases
  tar -xzf cloud-provider-kind_*_linux_amd64.tar.gz
  sudo install cloud-provider-kind /usr/local/bin/
  sudo cloud-provider-kind            (leave running in a terminal)

Then:
  k get svc backend-lb -w            # EXTERNAL-IP appears
  curl <EXTERNAL-IP>

This is what EKS/AKS/GKE do for you with a real cloud LB (Week 7).


